prompt (Keaton) kg build human-name custody with your design
think my design instinct held — a name is a signed claim by the point's keeper, unique in a small shared registry, resolvable both ways; and I built it as real custody (claim and release), not mere registration
think the deep point — names are the one surface that genuinely needs consensus; a point's keys ride the fast path in its Deed, but a name must be globally unique so everyone agrees who alice is, so the NameRegistry is honestly a shared/consensus structure, distinct from the owned Deeds
think authority is two-fold — to claim a name the caller must own the point (its Deed verifies against the constellation via the shrink's own verify) and the point's keeper must sign the exact name; a tag byte distinguishes a claim signature from a release, so one can never be replayed as the other
think validated names as short lowercase DNS-like labels (letters, digits, inner hyphens, 1..32) so a name can never be mistaken for an address or carry junk; the example names are plain words
obs GREEN on the first build; composed settlement.open/mint/verify and the tilak signing; resolve both ways works, and every refusal holds — a taken name, an already-named point, a non-owner ghost deed, a forged signature, a wrongful release, and malformed names that never parse
